---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SectionHead from "../components/SectionHead.astro";
import TierLedger from "../components/TierLedger.astro";
import AtlasExplorer from "../components/AtlasExplorer.astro";
import Stamp from "../components/Stamp.astro";
import Plate from "../components/Plate.astro";
import { SUMMARY, TIERS } from "../data/compat";
---

<Base
  title="Media Atlas"
  description={`Every format NOVA has been tested with and every one it hasn't: ${SUMMARY.samples} harness samples in software and hardware passes, plus documented Expected, vendor-SDK and unsupported formats.`}
>
  <section class="section head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead title="Media Atlas" as="h1" />
      <div class="head-grid">
        <div class="head-copy">
          <p class="lede">
            Look up a codec, a container or a file extension and see exactly what NOVA's engine was tested with, what it
            wasn't, and the evidence behind each answer.
          </p>
          <p class="facts num">
            {SUMMARY.samples} synthetic samples · {SUMMARY.softwareMet}/{SUMMARY.samples} met expectation in software ·
            {" "}{SUMMARY.hardwareMet}/{SUMMARY.samples} with hardware decoding allowed · {SUMMARY.decoders} decoders in the
            engine build
          </p>
        </div>
        <TierLedger />
      </div>
      <aside class="standing" aria-label="How to read the Atlas">
        <p class="spine">A recognised file is not a promise.</p>
        <p>
          MXF, MOV, MP4 and MKV are containers: the codec inside decides what plays. “Verified” means a synthetic sample of
          that profile played on the real engine, not a file from a camera. Real camera files, RAW formats and anything not
          listed are untested until they say otherwise.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tight index" aria-label="Formats">
    <div class="wrap">
      <AtlasExplorer />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section method sunk" aria-labelledby="method-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead id="method-h" title="How the evidence was made" />
      <div class="method-grid">
        <div class="method-copy">
          <p>
            Each sample is a short synthetic encode of one profile, made with FFmpeg by the repository's corpus script. The
            test harness opens it on the real playback engine ({SUMMARY.engine.mpv}, FFmpeg {SUMMARY.engine.ffmpeg}) with
            no window and no sound device, and checks that:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>playback starts, or fails the way the sample expects;</li>
            <li>codec, size, bit depth, chroma, alpha and HDR are detected correctly;</li>
            <li>an exact seek lands within 100 ms;</li>
            <li>the position advances in real time, with no decoder drops.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            It ran twice on one machine: Windows 11, Intel i5-10300H, Intel UHD 630 and NVIDIA GTX 1650. Once with hardware
            decoding off, once with it allowed. {SUMMARY.hardwareDecoded} samples were decoded on the GPU; the rest fell
            back to software automatically.
          </p>
          <p>
            What this does not cover: real camera files, long files, audio/video sync for professional formats (measured
            only for H.264, HEVC and VP9 in the live app), and how HDR looks on a real display.
          </p>
          <p><a class="link-arrow" href="/docs/compatibility">Read the full compatibility record</a></p>
        </div>
        <dl class="tiers">
          {
            TIERS.map((t) => (
              <div>
                <dt>
                  <Stamp tier={t.id} label={t.label} />
                </dt>
                <dd>{t.meaning}</dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div class="in-app">
        <Plate shot="formats" no="09" sizes="(min-width: 1100px) 50vw, 100vw" />
        <p class="muted">
          The same tiers appear inside NOVA, in Settings → Formats, so the app and this page give the same answer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-bottom: var(--s7);
  }
  .head-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 6fr);
    gap: var(--s7);
    align-items: end;
  }
  .head-copy {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s4);
  }
  .facts {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    max-width: 52ch;
  }
  .standing {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 4fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
    gap: var(--s3) var(--s7);
    margin-top: var(--s8);
    padding-top: var(--s5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--seam);
  }
  .standing .spine {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 1.2rem + 1.2vw, 2.2rem);
  }
  .standing p:last-child {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: 68ch;
  }
  .index {
    padding-top: 0;
  }
  .method-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 6fr) minmax(0, 5fr);
    gap: var(--s8);
  }
  .method-copy {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s4);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .method-copy ul {
    padding-left: 1.2em;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s1);
  }
  .tiers {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
    border-top: 1.5px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .tiers div {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s2);
    padding: var(--s4) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--seam);
  }
  .tiers dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .in-app {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 7fr) minmax(0, 4fr);
    gap: var(--s6);
    align-items: end;
    margin-top: var(--s8);
  }
  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .head-grid,
    .standing,
    .method-grid,
    .in-app {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
